# =============================================================================
# ctx by GottZ — Environment Configuration
# The memory your LLM pretends to have.
# https://github.com/GottZ/ctx | hire@gottz.de
#
# Copy to .env and fill in real values:
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Generate secrets:
#   openssl rand -hex 32          # 64-char hex string (API keys, passwords)
#   openssl rand -base64 32       # 44-char base64 string (encryption keys)
#   openssl rand -hex 16          # 32-char hex string (shorter secrets)
# =============================================================================

# --- PostgreSQL (admin) ---
POSTGRES_DB=n8n
POSTGRES_USER=admin
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME                 # openssl rand -hex 32

# --- PostgreSQL (application user) ---
POSTGRES_NON_ROOT_USER=n8n
POSTGRES_NON_ROOT_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME        # openssl rand -hex 32

# --- Context Store Database ---
CONTEXT_DB=context_store
CONTEXT_DB_USER=context_user
CONTEXT_DB_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME               # openssl rand -hex 32
# CONTEXT_DB_HOST=localhost                  # default: localhost (docker service name)
# CONTEXT_DB_PORT=5432                      # default: 5432
# CONTEXT_DB_SSLMODE=disable                # default: disable

# --- Sealbox Master Key ---
# Encrypts secrets at rest (OAuth provider keys etc). MUST be 64 hex chars
# (32 bytes) — Sealbox rejects non-hex, do NOT use base64 here.
# _PREV only while a key-rotation sweep is pending, remove afterwards.
CTX_SECRETS_KEY=CHANGE_ME                   # openssl rand -hex 32
# CTX_SECRETS_KEY_PREV=                     # previous key during rotation only

# --- First-Boot Admin Bootstrap ---
# On a FRESH (empty key table) DB there is no key to create the first key.
# Set this before the first boot: ctxd mints one server-admin key with this
# plaintext, then use it to create your real keys (see below). On a populated
# key table it is a no-op (fail-closed). Remove from .env after the first mint.
# CTX_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_KEY=                  # openssl rand -hex 32
# CTX_BOOTSTRAP_RUN_ID=                     # optional label suffix (e2e-bootstrap-<id>)

# --- Settings-Layer Kill Switch ---
# CTX_SETTINGS_DISABLE=1 ignores every context_settings DB override (boot AND
# reload) — break-glass when a bad override wedges the daemon. Env-only by
# design; leave unset in normal operation.
# CTX_SETTINGS_DISABLE=

# --- Context Store API Keys (test.sh inputs) ---
# NOT read by the server — test.sh sends them as X-Context-Key headers.
# Create the actual keys via the API using the bootstrap admin key above:
#   ctx keys create <label> --home <scope>  # then paste the plaintext here
CONTEXT_API_KEY_PRIVATE=CHANGE_ME           # ctx keys create (private home scope)
CONTEXT_API_KEY_WORK=CHANGE_ME              # ctx keys create (work home scope)

# --- Base URL ---
# Used by test.sh, eval.sh, ctx CLI, and scripts.
WEBHOOK_BASE_URL=https://ctx.example.com

# =============================================================================
# INFERENCE BACKENDS
#
# The backend pool (`context_backends`) is the living LLM configuration: one
# row per inference host, carrying the roles it serves (chat / synthesis /
# translate / digest / embed / dream / dream-embed / rerank), its protocol, its
# models and — for cloud providers — a reference to a sealed API key. Nothing
# in this file configures it. Seed it once, after the first boot:
#
#   ctx init                                  # interactive: config, pool, hooks
#
#   ctx backends seed --host http://gpu-host:11434 \
#       --model qwen3.5:9b --embed-model qwen3-embedding:8b
#
# Cloud providers are ordinary pool rows — `protocol` `openai`, plus a sealed
# secret instead of a plaintext key in this file:
#
#   echo "sk-ant-..." | ctx secrets set anthropic-chat
#   ctx backends seed --file seed.json         # per-role host/model/protocol/trust
#
# Keep embeddings local: changing the embedding model changes the vector space
# and needs a re-embed migration. Synthesis and Dream can be routed to any
# provider independently, one pool row each.
#
# Until the pool is seeded it stays empty: `/health` answers unhealthy (503)
# and the boot log names the state (`backend_pool: empty`). That is the
# designed fresh-install state, not a fault — `ctx init` / `ctx backends seed`
# is what ends it. The CONTAINER healthcheck stays green through that window
# on purpose: it judges serving, not greenness, so orchestrators do not gate
# away the very window in which the seed has to happen. Use `/health` or
# `ctx status`, not `docker ps`, to see whether the backends serve.
# Details: docs/operations.md#backends and docs/operations.md#first-run-ctx-init
# =============================================================================

# --- Pipeline knobs (not part of the backend pool) ---
# CTX_DREAM_ENABLED=true                    # default: false — background cross-referencing
# CTX_RERANK_ENABLED=false                  # default: false; with no pool row of role rerank the dispatch falls back to LLM-as-judge on the chat model
# CTX_TIMEZONE=Europe/Berlin                # default: UTC. For correct "heute"/"gestern" resolution.

# =============================================================================
# COMING FROM A v4.x .env?
#
# The backend tuple variables — the CTX_{CHAT,CHAT_FALLBACK,EMBED,DREAM,
# DREAM_EMBED,RERANK}_* connection settings that used to fill this file — are
# retired in v5.0.0. Nothing reads them, their settings keys answer 404 like
# any unknown key, and migration 133 removes leftover rows on them. Lines left
# behind in an existing .env do no harm; they are simply inert.
#
# They are not listed here on purpose. This template is copied verbatim into
# every new installation, and a fresh .env has no business carrying the
# vocabulary of a surface that no longer exists. The complete name list, the
# upgrade order (topology into the pool FIRST, verified with
# `ctx backends list`, env cleanup only AFTER the new binary runs) and the
# rollback caveats live in one place instead:
#
#   docs/operations.md#environment-variables
#
# Do not jump to v5.0.0 from an old v4: reach the last v4.x release —
# v4.38.0 — first. It is the last binary that still reads the tuple vars, and
# it names every one of them that is still set, so the pool can be filled from
# a running system rather than from memory.
# =============================================================================
